public class ErpCustomerSync {

    @future(callout=true)
    public static void syncAccountToErp(Id salesforceId, String name, String country) {
        // Build the JSON body for a single customer
        Map<String, Object> customer = new Map<String, Object>();
        customer.put('salesforceId', salesforceId);
        customer.put('name', name);
        customer.put('country', country);

        List<Map<String, Object>> customers = new List<Map<String, Object>>();
        customers.add(customer);

        String jsonBody = JSON.serialize(customers);

        // Make the callout using Named Credential
        Http http = new Http();
        HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();
        req.setEndpoint('callout:ERP/api/customers');
        req.setMethod('POST');
        req.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
        req.setBody(jsonBody);

        HttpResponse res = http.send(req);

        // Error handling and retries are out of scope
    }
}
```

**Deviation Note:** 
I used an `@future` method to make the callout from the trigger because direct callouts in triggers are not allowed. This meets the "one request per new account" requirement. However, `@future` has a limit of 100 calls per transaction, which could be exceeded when the marketplace integration creates batches of up to 200 accounts. In a production environment, this might need to be addressed with a batchable or queueable approach, but the requirement explicitly asks for one request per account and to make the callout "right from the trigger".

